WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.860
A thread is an execution path inside a process.

2
00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:15.960
A C# application can have many threads doing work at the same time, including request handling, background processing, timers, continuations, garbage collection support, and other runtime activity.

3
00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:22.060
Threading is powerful, but direct thread management is rarely the first tool application code should reach for.

4
00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:27.540
The .NET thread pool manages worker threads for many short-running operations.

5
00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:36.760
Instead of creating a new raw thread for every unit of work, application code usually schedules work that the runtime can execute on available worker threads.

6
00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:45.580
This helps the runtime reuse threads and balance throughput, but it also means blocked or long-running work can affect other work that depends on the same pool.

7
00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:49.540
Task represents asynchronous or parallel work.

8
00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:56.840
A task may complete after an I/O operation finishes, after CPU-bound work runs, or after a continuation is scheduled.

9
00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:08.140
The Task Parallel Library gives developers a structured way to compose work, observe completion, handle exceptions, and express concurrency without manually managing every thread.

10
00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:12.980
Parallelism and asynchrony are related but not identical.

11
00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:19.880
Parallelism uses multiple execution resources at the same time, usually to complete CPU-bound work faster.

12
00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:31.180
Asynchrony allows work to wait without blocking a thread, which is especially valuable for I/O-bound operations such as database calls, file operations, web requests, and message processing.

13
00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:35.460
async and await make asynchronous control flow easier to read.

14
00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:42.040
They allow a method to pause while waiting for a task and resume later without holding a thread the entire time.

15
00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:53.100
That improves scalability for many I/O-heavy server applications, but it does not remove the need to handle shared state, cancellation, exceptions, and resource limits.

16
00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:56.820
Choosing the wrong model creates practical problems.

17
00:01:56.920 --> 00:02:01.080
Creating raw threads for routine request work can waste resources.

18
00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:06.700
Treating CPU-heavy work as harmless async work can still overload processors.

19
00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:11.280
Blocking thread pool threads while waiting for I/O can reduce throughput.

20
00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:14.660
The design should match the kind of work being performed.
